// AN `i128` COLUMN IS A WORKING STATIC-TIER COLUMN AND CANNOT BE A RUNTIME ROW
// AT ALL — REFUSED BY WRIT, AT THE USER'S OWN LINE, BEFORE DEEM IS CONSULTED.
//
// WHAT IS PINNED, AND WHY THE LAYER IS HALF OF IT. `wql_domain_static_extremes`
// runs `select s.x` and `order by s.x` over an `i128` column at ±2^127 and both
// answer correctly, so the static tier admits the type. The dynamic and
// incremental tiers cannot see it: a schema field of type `i128` is not a
// `WritField` — the population at stdlib/lang/writ/wmap.logos:481-517, nineteen
// hand-written impls — so there is no T→WAny conversion and the program does not
// compile. The same refusal covers i128, u128, `String`, every user struct
// (with or without Hash), a fieldless enum, `(i64, str)` and `Option<i64>`: 220
// of the 1066 cells of the 2026-08-03 survey are this one sentence.
//
// ⚠ THIS IS THE GOOD SHAPE OF REFUSAL, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF PINNING THE
// LAYER RATHER THAN ONLY THE REDNESS. The diagnostic names the user's own file
// and line, the user's own function, the field by path (`r.x`), the type, and
// the missing capability by name. Compare its three siblings in this set —
// `wql_domain_layer_blob_struct_col_fail`, `..._blob_str_plus_fail` and
// `..._mlir_tuple_col_fail` — where the same class of question is refused from
// inside generated code or from inside MLIR, naming a function nobody wrote. A
// refusal that MOVES from here into one of those is a regression even though
// both are red, and only an assertion on the message can tell them apart.
//
// ⚠ THE EXPECTATION IS NOT THE DEFECT HERE — the refusal is correct as a
// refusal. What the survey calls a defect is the DISAGREEMENT it belongs to: one
// value domain admits i128 and another cannot represent it, so the same column
// is a first-class type to one engine and does not exist to the other two. When
// the value domain becomes the type system, this file's premise changes and the
// file should be rewritten to say what the new boundary is — not deleted.
package test;

use logos.lang.writ.container;
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;
use logos.lang.writ.array;
use logos.std.wql.ir;
use logos.std.wql.catalog_macro;
use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.deem;
use logos.lang.result;

pub schema R : code(0x0E68_0000_0000_0001) { id: i64 = 0, x: i128 = 1 }
resource cat = schema_catalog!{ R };

fn mkr(d: &Writ, id: i64, xv: i128) -> WAny {
    let r: R = d.make::<R>(); r.id = id; r.x = xv;
    return WAny::ref_to((r.m as i64) as *const u8);
}

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
